A||||21||Eric||0-60 for 3.2 rebuild||||9||
Z||000000||Eric||03-03-2000||09:53 PM||||3.2 rebuild<BR>-Federal Mogul hypereutectic pistons<BR>-ported heads<BR>-ported intake and exhaust manifolds<BR>-GM hipo. cam 427 lift 454 exhaust<BR>-high performance valve job<BR>-stiffer valve springs<BR>-adj. fuel reg set at 50psi<BR>-MSD coil<BR>-K&N filter element<BR>-timing set at roughly 13-14 degrees<P>88GT no weight reduction and 225/60/15 rear tires <P>0-60@ 8.1 seconds, quarter mile 16.2 measured by Gtech<P>was running in the mid 7 second range when the air temp was around 0-10 degrees F.<P>What's going on?  I'm out of ideas for making this engine perform the way it should.  The mods I listed above are all the mods I have.  Is there anything I'm missing that could free the motor up?  Exhaust, intake, ignition?<P>The car isn't much if any faster than stock with 130k miles.  The clutch doesn't slip a bit, it's brand new.  The performance #s are consistant with the kind of cars I can hang with in a race.  My friend's stock V6 dodge shadow is just a hair faster than the Fiero and I'm always getting toasted by buicks and hondas (slow 4 door versions).  I know this is a forum where guys like to brag about their fast cars, but my car isn't fast.  I want to brag too!||||reg||9
Z||000001||Eric||03-03-2000||09:54 PM||||It's a five speed too||||reg||8
Z||000002||fiero56||03-03-2000||10:08 PM||||0 to 60 in 8.1 with a 3.2 rebuild? I would definitly like to know what is going on, as I am planning to do the same with my Formula sometime next winter. Motoweek timed stock '88 Formula 0 to 60 in 7.5 seconds. They probably could have achieved lower, but this was what the average person could expect without thrashing the entire engine/drivetrain in several runs.<P>How many miles on the rebuild. It might just need a few miles to break in and run free-er.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-03-2000||10:11 PM||||a list of goodies done to my 2.8 with 96k on the clock... it will pull a friends 3.2 Fiero all day long! hes all kinds of mad about it but i can (George Palm of The Van SHop, here in atlanta) My car will SMOKE his 3.2. Yours Might just need proper tuning... where are you located? Is there a Dyno nearby? If so you might want to have them adjust the fuel pressure, timing and such... <A HREF="http://www.freeyellow.com/members/fiero/travis.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.freeyellow.com/members/fiero/travis.html</A>  (no nitrous 14.7 1/4mi at 87* Atlanta International Dragway) (86SE 4spd)<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 03-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||03-03-2000||10:24 PM||||What is the G-tech reading for rear wheel horse power, it should be a whole lot more than your stock 2.8? If it’s not than something is definitely wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||03-03-2000||10:25 PM||||There's about 4,500 miles on it now.  It's running on 92 octane. <P>I didn't just jump in the car yesterday.  I've had it for over a year and I'm pretty sure I know how to launch it and where to shift as well as almost anyone else.  I wish I could say the #s were a result of my lack of driving skills, but that would be stretching it.<P>A formula is quite a bit lighter than a GT, so you may have better luck than me.<P>I have to say, the motor seems to lose power at a lower rpm.  At 4500 there isn't much of anything left.  According to GM, the hipo cam is good for 6500 rpm with the proper valve springs (the ones I have).<P>56, before you get started you may want to consider a different route to gain power.  We should atleast figure out what the problem is before you or somebody else goes throwing a bunch of money in a pile.  This motor sucks!<P>Is there anyone out there with this setup or something similar who can shed some light on the hp problem?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||03-03-2000||10:33 PM||||When the engine is warm, do a compression test on a couple of the easy to get to cylinders. It sounds like you will have very low compression. You did not say how you achieved 3.2 liters. If you built the engine with a replacement crank and pistons, I'd guess that you have the wrong pistons for the heads. If this is not the case, cam timing may be way off. Check your throttle cable/body to ensure that the butterfly is fully open at WOT. That's all I can think of right now. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||03-03-2000||10:35 PM||||This may sound very elementary, but what heat range are you plugs running in?  I've seen some strange behavior with the wrong plugs.  Stock injectors?  Every try having someone rev your engine with a fuel pressure guage attached to see if it's starving?  Clogged cat?  Are you sure you've been jumpering the correct terminals when you time it????? let us know what you find.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||03-03-2000||10:39 PM||||I couldn't get an accurate reading on the Gtech for horsepower.  Sometimes it would read 90 or 375 just didn't seem to work too well.<P>I'm up in Columbus, Ohio.  I would like to use a dyno to tune the car up, but I'm worried I might just drop a ton of money with no real measurable results.  1FST2M6, I'm curious what your opinion is on the weak link in my motor.  Do you really think turning up or down the fuel pressure will really do that much?  Should I invest in some bigger injectors and have someone burn me a new chip?  The sad truth is, I'm not really enjoying this car.  It's really depressing to have to look at the taillamps of a dodge shadow and know there's nothing I can do about it.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000009||Eric||03-03-2000||10:51 PM||||I'm worried about the compression ratio too, Cooter.  The stock pistons are flat top, but the one's I got have dish.  It's not too deep, but it's definatly there.  I'm worried this is significantly lowering the compression.  The heads were milled .0010, but I doubt this could facilitate any real changes to the compression.<P>The plugs are off a stock 2.8.  I don't know what heat range they are.  Would gap matter?<P>Yes, a pressure gauge was attached to the shrader valve for pressure tests.  When revved the pressure remains at a constant 50 psi.  Is this normal?<P>I have cats.  They really like to lay on the rear trunk lid of the Fiero after it has been run.  I'm not sure if one of them is clogged.  To tell you the truth, I haven't thought to ask them.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||03-04-2000||12:16 AM||||Dished pistons might be considerably dropping compression. The cam timing is another possibilty, but I find it hard to believe someone spent all that time on the engine, and didn't get the degrees right. <P>A dodge Shadow is faster than you? Did it have turbo or something? I rode in a Shadow once, took longer than the highway onramp to hit 55mph. Just yesterday I had the pleasure of blowing away some guy in his 4-cyl Z3(rolling 20mph, no competition <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">).||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||03-04-2000||12:45 AM||||uum no more fuel is only the answer to nitrous or forced induction.. the dish piston might be a problem or a blessing for LOTS of boost! but like the others said, check your cat?  Get a set of NGK UR5 plugs (i've beat the crap out of my NGK and they seem to be doing well.. i replace them a lot just because but they ALWAYS look ok) Hook the car up to a scope and see if the O2 is working properly, and make sure your ignition control module is functioning right too... If not Accel makes a nice unit as does MSD and Blue Streak. have you unpluged the car latley and reset the computer? it may be in a type of "limp home" mode... my car will toss it's self into this state after a good nitrous flogging...(my computer is hardwired to a seperate switch just for this purpose) reset it and see if that makes any difference. i'm not real sure after that.. I've not run into any problem like that... I KILL O2s a lot though but thats cause i'm dumping fuel into it... you might have a "Lazy" O2 go to NAPA get part #OS101 like $20 that might help. if you just want to throw parts at it if you don't know somone with a scope. (many shops use a SUN scope) or a (Matco Reality) both can tell you if your O2 has issues and check your ICM in the process. keep me posted! ||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||03-04-2000||12:46 AM||||You might have AL head pistons. Do you know the part number?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phaeton||03-04-2000||01:24 AM||||Aluminum heads have tiny cumbustion chambers and use dished pistons.  Cast iron heads have big chambers and use flattop or domed pistons.<BR>The cam could also be running a few degrees advanced, that would play heck with the top end.  Sometimes they get sold when not perfect.<BR>When the pedal is floored is the butterfly ALL the way open? it takes two people to check this one.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||03-04-2000||09:08 AM||||Start with the basics. Is it getting good spark and plenty of fuel? Sounds like you've already checked the fuel, that's good. What kind of spark plug wires and are they new? Those Fiero engines seem to chew up plug wires regularly, maybe because of the extra heat under the deck lid, I don't know.<P>Next do a full compression check. This should of been done long time ago. Maybe a ring didn't seal or a valve is messed up. Is it burning any oil? Triple check the timing using the proper proceedure. Are you using the old rocker arms? They may have enough flex in the sidewalls to cause problems at high rpm. That motor should still pull fairly strong at 5 grand, I would NOT ever go much past 5,500 rpm (6 grand redline) with hydraulic lifters and pushrods. Actually, you start to run out of torque by about 5,000, so that's a good shift point.<P>If you have the wrong pistons which means running a compression ratio of maybe 8:1 or so, rather than 9:1 (ok, technically 8.9:1), then that might explain why you run out of torque at a lower rpm. However, low compression engines are ideal candidates for forced induction. Just slap a 75 shot of nitrous on that baby, it'll all right. You'll be back to laughing and winning races.||||reg||1
Z||000015||ChadMan||03-04-2000||09:22 AM||||Eric, I really wish I had a solution for you.  But I had to thank you for the belly laugh I had about your potentially-clogged cats lying on your deck lid.  HA x 1000||||reg||10
Z||000016||Eric||03-04-2000||09:58 AM||||Pistons are Federal Mogul H562P for '90 camaro.  These are the pistons Stroker told me to buy.<P>O2 sensor is new and I've got it hooked up to an AIR/FUEL gauge.<P>Plugs are new AC rapid fires.  I check them all the time for aluminum deposits.<P>Wires are new Accel super stock 8mm.<P>Cap and rotor are new also.<P>Rocker arms are new 1.5 ratio units.<P>The Dodge Shadow is a six cylinder rated at 140 horsepower.  A stock Fiero shouldn't have much trouble with this car.  He frequently gets beat by V6 stangs and camaros.<P>Unfortunaly, I didn't put the short block together myself so I'm not 100 percent that the cam was degreed correctly.  I asked my machinist if he degreed it.  He said yes, and showed me the spec sheet he used to do it.<P>I don't want to put a turbo on right now because I feel something is already wrong with the engine.  I'd guess that it's making something like 130-140 horsepower and close to 200 ft/lbs of torque.  ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Shaun41178||03-05-2000||06:58 PM||||You do have the correct pistons as they are for iron head 3.1's and are for compression ratio of 8.9-1 or so I have been told.  I don't know the compression ratio of 3.1's in Firebirds but it might be even 8.5-1 so these pistons could provide 8.5-1 compression.  But that still won't account for such low hp numbers.  Maybe 5-10 total.<P>My guess is the cam.  You said it runs out at like anything over 4500 rpm.  This cam was made to make power mid and upper range rpm.  This cam is designed to make goo dpower at 4500 and above unlike the stock Fiero cam.  I bet it was degreed wrong.  I am going to guess that your machinist degreed the cam like you would degree a SBC cam and it can't be done that way.  There is a special way for the 60* V6.  Ok well maybe not special but different and your machinist might have thought that it was the same as a SBC.  I think that the cam is degreed -4 really bringing the power down.  This cam should not be falling on its face at that rpm.  This probably won't help much but advance the ignition as much as you can untill you hear some pinging and then back off two degrees and go take it for a drive and see what that does.<P>I just thought of something else.  Perhaps the harmonic balancer is the cause.  Ed Parks said the harmonic balancer marks for timing are located in different spotsfrom a 2.8 and 3.1 damper.  I don't know this myself but that is what Ed said.  So maybe your timing is really retarded but you think it is advance 3 degrees from the base timing of ten.  So like I said go out and advance the timing more and see what the does.  If the cam is degreed wrong then this won't make much of a difference but maybe it will.  I must say I am not an expert on the functions of how a degreed cam of -4 or +4 effect performance.<P>I hope some of this info helps a bit.  <P>Eric I live 2 hours from you and wouldn't mind driving down and try to help diagnose the problem.  I work just about everyday this week but Tue.  Weather is supposed to be nice that day, like 70's I am told.  I could come down that day I guess.  Let me know.  I hope you see this before Monday night so you won't have to make a quick decision.  I will bring my g-tech as well to see if yours is reading right.  Respond to teh forum or just email me.  Shaun41178@aol.com  <P>Later.||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||03-06-2000||07:05 PM||||What's the O2 read at WOT? Above 800mV?<P>I believe those pistons are 8.5:1. So far, I've only found the Silvolite 1486's for 3.1L iron head at 8.8:1.<P>Head milling time. Or blow on the engine.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-07-2000).]||||reg||1
